{
  "id": "a1-19",
  "kind": "lesson",
  "level": "A1",
  "order": 25,
  "title": "Impersonal Constructions",
  "summary": "Sentences with no subject at all: the weather, how a person feels, what hurts, and what is permitted. Lithuanian says these with a special form of the adjective that agrees with nothing, and with the dative already met at ¶69.",
  "rules": [
    {
      "id": "neuter-adjective",
      "number": "126",
      "heading": "The Non-Gendered Adjective",
      "statement": "Beside its masculine and feminine forms every qualitative adjective has a third, **non-gendered** form — the *negimininė forma*. It is made from the masculine by changing **-as** to **-a** and **-us** to **-u**, and it agrees with **nothing**, because in these sentences there is nothing for it to agree with. It is the form used when a state is reported without any person or thing being said to be in it.",
      "paradigms": [
        {
          "caption": "The three forms of the adjective",
          "columns": [
            "masculine",
            "feminine",
            "non-gendered",
            "English"
          ],
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "cold",
              "cells": [
                "šaltas",
                "šalta",
                "šalta",
                "cold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "warm",
              "cells": [
                "šiltas",
                "šilta",
                "šilta",
                "warm"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "good",
              "cells": [
                "geras",
                "gera",
                "gera",
                "good"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "interesting",
              "cells": [
                "įdomus",
                "įdomi",
                "įdomu",
                "interesting"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "difficult",
              "cells": [
                "sunkus",
                "sunki",
                "sunku",
                "difficult"
              ]
            }
          ],
          "footnote": "For adjectives in **-as** the non-gendered form is spelt like the feminine, and only the sentence tells them apart. For those in **-us** the two are distinct — *įdomi* against *įdomu* — and the difference is plain to see."
        }
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Šiandien šalta.",
          "gloss": "It is cold today.",
          "note": "no subject at all"
        },
        {
          "target": "Čia labai gera.",
          "gloss": "It is very pleasant here."
        },
        {
          "target": "Kaip įdomu!",
          "gloss": "How interesting!"
        },
        {
          "target": "Man gerai.",
          "gloss": "I am fine. / I feel well."
        },
        {
          "target": "Jam gerai, bet man ne.",
          "gloss": "He is fine, but I am not."
        }
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "adjective-agreement",
        "adverb-formation"
      ],
      "core": true,
      "footnotes": []
    },
    {
      "id": "personal-vs-impersonal",
      "number": "127",
      "heading": "Personal against Impersonal",
      "statement": "This is the distinction on which the whole section turns. **Filmas įdomus** says that the film *is* interesting — a subject, and an adjective agreeing with it. **Man įdomu** says that *I find it* interesting — no subject, the person in the **dative**, and the non-gendered form. English uses the same word for both and gives no warning; Lithuanian keeps them wholly apart.",
      "paradigms": [
        {
          "caption": "Two sentences that English confuses",
          "columns": [
            "Lithuanian",
            "English",
            "construction"
          ],
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "personal",
              "cells": [
                "Filmas įdomus.",
                "The film is interesting.",
                "subject + agreeing adjective"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "impersonal",
              "cells": [
                "Man įdomu.",
                "I find it interesting.",
                "dative + non-gendered form"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "personal",
              "cells": [
                "Ji šalta.",
                "She is cold (a cold person).",
                "said of her character"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "impersonal",
              "cells": [
                "Jai šalta.",
                "She feels cold.",
                "said of her sensation"
              ]
            }
          ],
          "footnote": "*Ji šalta* and *jai šalta* differ by two letters and mean quite different things — the first describes what she is like, the second what she feels. It is worth reading the pair aloud until the difference is automatic."
        }
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Tomui šalta.",
          "gloss": "Tomas feels cold."
        },
        {
          "target": "Mums buvo labai gera.",
          "gloss": "We had a very good time.",
          "note": "the past of *būti* carries the impersonal sentence into the past"
        },
        {
          "target": "Ar tau sunku?",
          "gloss": "Is it difficult for you?"
        }
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "neuter-adjective",
        "dative-pronouns",
        "past-buti"
      ],
      "core": true,
      "footnotes": []
    },
    {
      "id": "weather-verbs",
      "number": "128",
      "heading": "The Weather",
      "statement": "The weather is reported either with a non-gendered adjective — *šalta*, *šilta*, *karšta* — or with a verb that has **no subject and never will have one**. *Lyja* is *it is raining*; there is no word answering to the English *it*, and supplying one is wrong. These verbs exist only in the third person.",
      "paradigms": [
        {
          "caption": "Weather without a subject",
          "columns": [
            "Lithuanian",
            "English",
            "kind"
          ],
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "rain",
              "cells": [
                "Lyja.",
                "It is raining.",
                "verb"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "snow",
              "cells": [
                "Sninga.",
                "It is snowing.",
                "verb"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "cold",
              "cells": [
                "Šalta.",
                "It is cold.",
                "non-gendered adjective"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "hot",
              "cells": [
                "Karšta.",
                "It is hot.",
                "non-gendered adjective"
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Vakar lijo visą dieną.",
          "gloss": "Yesterday it rained all day.",
          "note": "the past of an impersonal verb, still third person"
        },
        {
          "target": "Rytoj bus šilta.",
          "gloss": "Tomorrow it will be warm."
        },
        {
          "target": "Žiemą dažnai sninga.",
          "gloss": "In winter it often snows."
        }
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "neuter-adjective",
        "future-buti",
        "accusative-of-time"
      ],
      "core": true,
      "footnotes": []
    },
    {
      "id": "skaudeti",
      "number": "129",
      "heading": "What Hurts: Skaudėti",
      "statement": "**Skaudėti** is impersonal and takes two things: the sufferer in the **dative**, and the part that hurts in the **accusative**. English makes the part the subject — *my head aches* — and Lithuanian makes it the object of a verb with no subject at all. This is the sentence a learner needs at the doctor's, and it is worth learning whole.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Man skauda galvą.",
          "gloss": "My head aches.",
          "note": "literally: to-me it-hurts the-head"
        },
        {
          "target": "Jai skauda koją.",
          "gloss": "Her leg hurts."
        },
        {
          "target": "Ar tau skauda?",
          "gloss": "Does it hurt?"
        },
        {
          "target": "Vakar man skaudėjo dantį.",
          "gloss": "Yesterday I had toothache."
        }
      ],
      "footnotes": [
        "Do not say *mano galva skauda*: the possessive and the nominative both belong to the English construction, not the Lithuanian one."
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "dative-pronouns",
        "accusative-nouns",
        "reikia"
      ],
      "core": true
    },
    {
      "id": "galima-reikia",
      "number": "130",
      "heading": "What Is Necessary and What Is Allowed",
      "statement": "**Reikia** was given at ¶69; it is one of a small family. **Galima** is *it is possible, one may*; **negalima** is *it is forbidden*; **verta** is *it is worth while*. All are impersonal, all are completed by an **infinitive**, and all take the person concerned in the **dative** if he is mentioned at all. Because they are impersonal they have no personal endings — they change only for tense.",
      "paradigms": [
        {
          "caption": "The impersonals and their tenses",
          "columns": [
            "present",
            "past",
            "future",
            "English"
          ],
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "necessity",
              "cells": [
                "reikia",
                "reikėjo",
                "reikės",
                "it is necessary"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "permission",
              "cells": [
                "galima",
                "buvo galima",
                "bus galima",
                "one may"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "prohibition",
              "cells": [
                "negalima",
                "nebuvo galima",
                "nebus galima",
                "one may not"
              ]
            }
          ],
          "footnote": "*Galima* is itself a non-gendered adjective pressed into service as a verb, which is why it takes *buvo* and *bus* to change its tense rather than endings of its own."
        }
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Čia galima rūkyti?",
          "gloss": "May one smoke here?"
        },
        {
          "target": "Ne, čia negalima.",
          "gloss": "No, it is not allowed here."
        },
        {
          "target": "Man reikėjo dirbti.",
          "gloss": "I had to work."
        },
        {
          "target": "Rytoj reikės anksti keltis.",
          "gloss": "Tomorrow it will be necessary to get up early."
        }
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "reikia",
        "complementary-infinitive",
        "neuter-adjective"
      ],
      "core": true,
      "footnotes": []
    },
    {
      "id": "impersonal-summary",
      "number": "131",
      "heading": "Recognising an Impersonal Sentence",
      "statement": "Three marks together identify one: there is **no nominative**, the verb or adjective stands in a form that **agrees with nothing**, and the person, if there is one, is in the **dative**. Where English inserts a dummy *it* — *it is cold*, *it is necessary*, *it hurts* — Lithuanian inserts nothing, and the absence is the construction.",
      "paradigms": [
        {
          "caption": "English dummy subject, Lithuanian nothing",
          "columns": [
            "English",
            "Lithuanian",
            "what is absent"
          ],
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "weather",
              "cells": [
                "It is raining.",
                "Lyja.",
                "no *it*"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "sensation",
              "cells": [
                "I am cold.",
                "Man šalta.",
                "no *I* as subject"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "necessity",
              "cells": [
                "I have to work.",
                "Man reikia dirbti.",
                "no *I* as subject"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "pain",
              "cells": [
                "My head aches.",
                "Man skauda galvą.",
                "no possessive, no subject"
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Buvo šalta, todėl likome namie.",
          "gloss": "It was cold, so we stayed at home.",
          "note": "the first clause impersonal, the second personal"
        },
        {
          "target": "Man gera čia gyventi.",
          "gloss": "I like living here."
        }
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "personal-vs-impersonal",
        "galima-reikia",
        "nes-todel"
      ],
      "core": true,
      "footnotes": []
    }
  ],
